WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.720
Let’s get some answers to these two situations.&nbsp;
What did you come up with for what line 2 will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:13.880
print? Line 2 is a slicing statement,&nbsp;
as we have square brackets on the quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:20.320
and the slice starts at character 4 and goes to&nbsp;
character 9. In looking at the quote, I think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:27.160
word quick should display, as the q is in position&nbsp;
4, and we are looking to display the next five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:35.800
characters. We will run this code in a moment.&nbsp;
Regarding the question on line 4 as to what type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.920
of data structure this is, this data structure&nbsp;
is a tuple, as the items in this list are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.640
parentheses. Sets and dictionaries have items in&nbsp;
curly brackets, and lists have items in square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:54.120
brackets. The next question is, can we change&nbsp;
the value of platinum to bronze? Technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.120 --> 00:01:01.240
we can, but we cannot do it just by changing a&nbsp;
value, as I am attempting to do here, on line 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.600
as tuples are immutable, meaning they cannot be&nbsp;
changed. The only way to make the change is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:17.600
redo the tuple, as I will do on line 6, with the&nbsp;
new value. And then I will add a print statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:30.120
on line 7 to display the new tuple values. I will&nbsp;
now run the code, and both results display in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:37.320
terminal. Knowing how to manipulate lists is a big&nbsp;
time-saver as, just like we saw in creating lists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.240
being able to group values in a single variable&nbsp;
and then make changes to the group saves one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.400
from having to create and store separate values in&nbsp;
separate variables, which can be tough to manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:53.560
given every variable has to have a different name.&nbsp;
Imagine trying to store 50 state capitals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:59.840
having to name the variables capital1 through&nbsp;
capital50. That list would be tough to track.

